before:
This complete example makes both changes visible before choosing which part to undo.
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo_accounts(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,balance INTEGER);
INSERT INTO demo_accounts VALUES(1,10),(2,20);
BEGIN;
UPDATE demo_accounts SET balance=15
WHERE id=1;
SAVEPOINT trial;
UPDATE demo_accounts SET balance=99
WHERE id=2;
ROLLBACK TO trial;
RELEASE trial;
COMMIT;
SELECT id,balance
FROM demo_accounts
ORDER BY id;
```
The final rows are 1/15 and 2/20: the first change survives, the later trial change does not.

after:
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo_accounts(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, balance INTEGER);
INSERT INTO demo_accounts VALUES (1, 10), (2, 20);
BEGIN;
UPDATE demo_accounts SET balance = 15 WHERE id = 1;
SAVEPOINT trial;
UPDATE demo_accounts SET balance = 99 WHERE id = 2;
ROLLBACK TO trial;
RELEASE trial;
COMMIT;
SELECT id, balance FROM demo_accounts ORDER BY id;
```
Account 1 ends at 15. Account 2 ends at 20. The first change happened before the mark; the second change happened after it.
